Report RA02752: Pop Britain - Site Visit - Pop Britain - Site Visit (2025-11-21)
Analysts: Charlie Higgs
Companies: Coca-Cola Europacific Partners
Sectors: Consumer: Beverages
Publication type: Blue Top
Published date: 2025-11-21
Section: The road ahead > Better competition in sparkling

In 2025, we have seen a raft of new innovation launches by Carlsberg-Britvic in Great Britain to support the sparkling portfolio:

- Pepsi Zero Sugar Strawberries ‘N’ Cream Soda and Cream Soda launched in January 2025 (these were launched in the US first and have now been launched in the UK, but the products were not rebranded to the UK’s Pepsi ‘Max’);

- Tango Strawberry Smash launched in February 2025;

- Aqua Libre Watermelon & Strawberry launched in February 2025;

- Tango Cherry Blast and Raspberry Blast returned in April 2025 as limited edition releases;

- 7-UP Pink Lemonade launched in April 2025;

- London Essence Crisp Apple Crafted Soda launched in April 2025, mirroring Fever-Tree’s strategy to expand the mixer range into premium adult soft drinks;

- Purdey’s (Britvic’s ‘natural’ energy drink brand) underwent a brand refresh in September 2025, coupled with two new flavour launches: Berry & Dragon Fruit and Orange & Mango;

- Pepsi Zero Sugar went incredibly early and launched a Christmas-inspired Gingerbread flavour on 1 October 2025.

So far, we are encouraged by Carlsberg-Britvic’s innovation launches – this is healthy competition that should support growth for both the company and the overall sparkling category.

This is in contrast to last year, ahead of the acquisition, when we observed some deep promotions to drive volume growth. For example, in Iceland there was a longstanding mix-and-match promotion for 72x 330ml cans of Pepsi Max and/or Tango for £24 in the grocery channel, equivalent to just 10 pence per 100ml (Fig 39).
